WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.707
Secrets: where the key lives.

2
00:00:01.987 --> 00:00:18.200
The Twelve-Factor App’s rule is the usual starting point: config, including credentials, varies between deploys and belongs in the environment, not the code; its test is whether you could open-source the repository right now without leaking a credential.

3
00:00:18.480 --> 00:00:31.941
OWASP’s Secrets Management Cheat Sheet adds the caveats: centralize secrets in a managed store, rotate them, scope each one to what needs it, keep them out of source code, Docker images, and CI logs, scan for leaks,

4
00:00:32.221 --> 00:00:38.749
and remember that environment variables are visible to every process and can end up in logs and crash dumps.

5
00:00:39.029 --> 00:00:43.808
Each example platform has a secret store that injects values at runtime.

6
00:00:44.088 --> 00:00:55.779
Vercel environment variables are encrypted at rest and can be typed as Config or Secret, where Secret values are write-only after saving; changes apply only to new deployments.

7
00:00:56.059 --> 00:01:03.717
Cloudflare secrets are set with wrangler secret put, hidden after creation, and read from the Worker’s env binding, with a.

8
00:01:03.997 --> 00:01:04.701
dev.

9
00:01:04.981 --> 00:01:05.877
vars or.

10
00:01:06.157 --> 00:01:08.824
env file (git-ignored) for local development.

11
00:01:09.104 --> 00:01:23.333
Cloud Run reads from Google Secret Manager, either as an environment variable resolved when the instance starts (the docs recommend pinning a version) or as a mounted volume read at access time, which suits rotation.

12
00:01:23.613 --> 00:01:33.171
On the Anthropic side, the authentication docs recommend storing keys in a secrets manager, rotating them, and disabling any key you suspect has leaked.

13
00:01:33.451 --> 00:01:45.781
They distinguish personal keys (for your own development) from service account keys (for shared and production workloads, so the key doesn’t break when a person leaves), let you set an expiration when you create a key,

14
00:01:46.061 --> 00:01:59.203
and describe Workload Identity Federation for production workloads on cloud platforms: the service exchanges its cloud identity for a short-lived token, so there is no long-lived API key to leak at all.

15
00:01:59.483 --> 00:02:06.288
Prefer federation where your platform supports it, and a scoped service account key from a secret store where it doesn’t.
